<h1>CI/CD Integration</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">GitHub Actions integration, SARIF output for code scanning, monitor modes, and exit code handling.</p>

    <h2 id="github-action">GitHub Actions</h2>

    <p>The simplest way to add vexes to your CI pipeline is the official composite action. By default it is <strong style="color: var(--text-1);">output-first</strong>: the action succeeds and exposes finding counts as outputs for you to branch on, and only blocks the build when you opt in with <code>fail-on-findings</code>.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># .github/workflows/vexes.yml</span>
<span class="kw">name</span>: Dependency Security
<span class="kw">on</span>: [pull_request, push]

<span class="kw">jobs</span>:
  <span class="kw">scan</span>:
    <span class="kw">runs-on</span>: ubuntu-latest
    <span class="kw">steps</span>:
      - <span class="kw">uses</span>: actions/checkout@v4
      - <span class="kw">uses</span>: penumbraforge/vexes@v0
        <span class="kw">id</span>: vexes
        <span class="kw">with</span>:
          <span class="kw">command</span>: scan
          <span class="kw">severity</span>: high
          <span class="kw">fail-on-findings</span>: <span class="val">'true'</span>   <span class="cmt"># opt-in: block the build on findings</span></div>

    <h3>Inputs</h3>

    <table class="cmd-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Input</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>command</code></td><td>scan</td><td><code>scan</code>, <code>analyze</code>, or <code>monitor</code>. <code>guard</code> is rejected — it performs real installs and is never permitted from CI.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>severity</code></td><td>high</td><td>Minimum severity to report: low, moderate, high, critical</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>path</code></td><td>.</td><td>Path to scan</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sarif-file</code></td><td>(none)</td><td>Also write a SARIF 2.1.0 report to this path, for <code>github/codeql-action/upload-sarif</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>fail-on-findings</code></td><td>false</td><td>When <code>true</code>, the action fails the build on findings (exit 1) or an incomplete scan (exit 2). Off by default so outputs reach the caller — a composite action that fails does not expose its outputs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>node-version</code></td><td>24</td><td>Node.js version to run vexes with</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>args</code></td><td>(none)</td><td>Additional arguments passed through to vexes (use only trusted values)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Outputs</h3>

    <table class="cmd-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Output</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>vulnerabilities</code></td><td>Total vulnerabilities (or flagged packages) at or above the severity threshold</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>critical</code></td><td>Number of CRITICAL findings</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>high</code></td><td>Number of HIGH findings</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>moderate</code></td><td>Number of MODERATE findings</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>exit-code</code></td><td>vexes exit code (0 clean, 1 findings, 2 incomplete/error)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sarif-file</code></td><td>Path to the written SARIF report, if <code>sarif-file</code> was requested</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="info-box">
      <strong>Hardened by design.</strong> Every input is passed to the run step through quoted environment variables — nothing is template-interpolated into the shell, so the action is not a template-injection sink. It also runs the pinned action code shipped in the <code>uses:</code> ref, never fetching vexes from the registry at runtime.
    </div>

    <h2 id="monitor-ci">Monitor --ci Mode</h2>

    <p>The <code>monitor --ci</code> flag optimizes output for CI environments:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Emits GitHub Actions annotation commands (<code>::error</code>, <code>::warning</code>) so findings appear inline on the PR diff.</li>
      <li>Exits with code 1 if findings exceed the severity threshold, failing the check.</li>
      <li>Exits with code 2 if the scan is incomplete (network failure, API timeout). This prevents false clean reports.</li>
      <li>Disables interactive prompts and color codes that break CI logs.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">vexes monitor --ci --severity high</span>

<span class="dim">::error file=package-lock.json::CRITICAL: axios 1.14.1 — MAINTAINER_CHANGE (publishing account changed 2 days ago)
::warning file=package-lock.json::HIGH: loadash 4.17.99 — TYPOSQUAT (edit distance 1 from "lodash")</span></div>

    <h2 id="sarif">SARIF Output</h2>

    <p>Set the <code>sarif-file</code> input to write a SARIF (Static Analysis Results Interchange Format) 2.1.0 report, then pair it with <code>github/codeql-action/upload-sarif</code> to surface findings on the Security tab.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># .github/workflows/vexes.yml</span>
<span class="kw">name</span>: Dependency Security
<span class="kw">on</span>: [pull_request, push]

<span class="kw">jobs</span>:
  <span class="kw">scan</span>:
    <span class="kw">runs-on</span>: ubuntu-latest
    <span class="kw">permissions</span>:
      <span class="kw">security-events</span>: write
    <span class="kw">steps</span>:
      - <span class="kw">uses</span>: actions/checkout@v4
      - <span class="kw">uses</span>: penumbraforge/vexes@v0
        <span class="kw">id</span>: vexes
        <span class="kw">with</span>:
          <span class="kw">command</span>: scan
          <span class="kw">severity</span>: high
          <span class="kw">sarif-file</span>: vexes.sarif
      - <span class="kw">name</span>: Upload SARIF
        <span class="kw">uses</span>: github/codeql-action/upload-sarif@v3
        <span class="kw">if</span>: always()
        <span class="kw">with</span>:
          <span class="kw">sarif_file</span>: vexes.sarif</div>

    <h2 id="behavioral-analysis-ci">Behavioral Analysis in CI</h2>

    <p>For deeper protection, run behavioral analysis alongside vulnerability scanning. Use <code>analyze --strict</code> to fail the build on any behavioral signal.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="kw">steps</span>:
  - <span class="kw">name</span>: Vulnerability scan
    <span class="kw">run</span>: npx @penumbraforge/vexes scan --severity high
  - <span class="kw">name</span>: Behavioral analysis
    <span class="kw">run</span>: npx @penumbraforge/vexes analyze --strict</div>

    <p>The <code>--strict</code> flag causes <code>analyze</code> to exit with code 1 on any signal, regardless of severity. For PR checks where you want to enforce zero behavioral anomalies, this is the right setting.</p>

    <h2 id="watch-mode">Watch Mode</h2>

    <p>For local development, <code>monitor --watch</code> continuously monitors lockfiles and re-scans when changes are detected. It also polls OSV periodically for new advisories affecting your current dependencies.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">vexes monitor --watch</span>

<span class="dim">  Watching: package-lock.json, Cargo.lock
  OSV poll interval: 1 hour
  Press Ctrl+C to stop.</span>

<span class="dim">  [14:32:01] Lockfile changed: package-lock.json
  [14:32:02] Scanning 315 packages...
  [14:32:04] Clean. No vulnerabilities found.</span>

<span class="dim">  [15:32:04] OSV poll — checking for new advisories...
  [15:32:05] Clean. No new advisories.</span></div>

    <h2 id="exit-codes">Exit Codes for CI</h2>

    <table class="cmd-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Code</th><th>CI Meaning</th><th>Recommended Action</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Pass</td><td>No findings above threshold. Build proceeds.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>Fail</td><td>Findings detected. Block merge until resolved.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>Error</td><td>Scan incomplete. Do not trust the result. Investigate.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="info-box">
      <strong>Exit code 2 matters.</strong> If your CI ignores exit code 2, a network failure during scanning will be treated as "clean" -- which is exactly what an attacker would want. Always handle exit code 2 as a build failure.
    </div>
